00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.060
Which is exactly what we are covering in today's strategy spotlight. Quality triage and challenge

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:50.260
sectors means systematically ranking names instead of avoiding the entire sector. The ranking uses

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.760
leverage below 1.1 times, investment grade status, portfolio scale, market to model realism,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.900
and premium to net asset value. This identifies the best of a bad lot when headlines look ugly.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.940
Today's conditions make it relevant because current parallels to the 1970s are harder to ignore.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:13.340
Committee debate now centers on admitting rate cuts are off the table. In practice,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.680
you screen business development companies using those leverage and rating filters.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:25.240
For example, the largest publicly traded one has a $12.8 billion market cap and a $22 billion investment

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:33.060
portfolio. It runs at 1.05 times leverage in ranks highest among 39 names despite 100% private credit

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.780
exposure. This approach worked best during late 1970s repricing cycles when quality names survived

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.660
sector wide markdowns. Higher leverage peers got hit first in those environments. Risks include net

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:50.480
asset value lagging reality by one to two quarters. Accumulated losses can appear only in the second

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.020
or third quarter reports. The strategy therefore demands strict position sizing and readiness to

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:02.100
reduce exposure on any sector wide liquidity squeeze. The one line takeaway is quality mitigates but

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.040
does not eliminate sector headwinds when marked to model accounting delays loss recognition.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.320
This same discipline of looking past headline prices carries over into how we should think about

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.060
dividends and special distributions. A lesson many retail investors learned the hard way.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:25.120
Imagine you had been watching STNE around $14 only to see it issue a $2 per share special dividend

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.680
after selling part of its business. The quoted price dropped to roughly $11 to $12.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:35.920
Many retail investors immediately think they are getting a better entry with a lower break-even point.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.760
What actually happens is that on the ex-dividend date, the opening price is mechanically reduced

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.760
by the dividend amount. All else equal, the prior owner receives the cash while the new buyer

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:52.000
purchases the company at its now smaller asset base. Your total economic position is unchanged.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:57.840
In Canadian tax advantage to counts like a TFSA, the $2 dividend is received tax-free.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.480
Yet the adjusted cost base for capital gains' purposes drops by the dividend amount on special

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:09.420
distributions. Platforms and quote feeds automatically adjust the displayed price, but they rarely flag

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.200
the simultaneous change in the company's enterprise value. The biggest misconception is treating

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.740
the post-dividend lower share price as an automatic bargain, without recalculating valuation multiples

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:26.320
on the reduced asset base. Always compare pre and post-dividend enterprise value over EBITDA or price

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.360
to sales on the core remaining business, adjust your TFSA or RRSP position size accordingly.

